{% figura src="/images/vignettes/issa-10.png" alt="Line drawing of a trapezoidal booth plan with figures crowded at its narrow end" /%}

*Pirate Radios: The radio created out of the necessity of the Rolling Stones, used today for underground and international music all over the world.*

The pirate radio manifests itself in many forms, most characterized by its ability to adapt. Pirate radio began in the early 1960s off the coast of Western Europe, most notably in the North Sea near Great Britain, Denmark, and the Netherlands. It started as a direct rebellion against government-controlled broadcasting monopolies, like the BBC, which majorly limited the airplay of modern rock, pop, rhythm and blues music. To bypass strict national laws and licensing regulations, enterprising broadcasters mounted powerful transmitters onto abandoned World War II anti-aircraft forts and anchored ships in international waters. The movement officially took off on Easter 1964 with the launch of Radio Caroline, an offshore radio ship that broadcasted non-stop popular music to millions of listeners. The actual radio room itself was shaped like a captain’s seat, but rather than steering a crew through the sea, the moderator guides the listeners through tracks.

It was a cramped booth with no wasted space- no space for standing; a booth that could be either broadcasting or receiving distress signals to and from other ships. It seemed to overflow with technological equipment; Do the dials control the music, or are they just instruments for navigation? Young men with long hair dressed casually in 60s style clothing were almost always pictured in the space.

Today, cities like New York, Miami, and London have the densest pirate radio ecosystems. Studies by broadcasting associations have historically shown that certain neighborhoods in NYC have more active pirate stations on the dial than legal ones. Many modern pirates broadcast to specific immigrant communities, offering news, talk shows, and music in minority languages (such as Haitian Creole, Spanish, or Jamaican Patois) that corporate radio doesn’t usually operate in.

